var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"custom_output/#Custom-Receiver-Output","page":"Custom Receiver Output","text":"By default receive emits a compact ReceiverDataOfInterest for every processed chunk: a sat_data dictionary (each satellite's CN0, prompt correlator value and health), the current pvt solution and the runtime. That covers most needs, and since pvt is the full PositionVelocityTime.PVTSolution a lot more is already reachable from it (velocity, DOP, clock drift — see Worked Example (Real Data)).\n\nWhen you need something the summary does not carry — the raw carrier Doppler, code phase or carrier phase of the tracking loops, or the decoded navigation data — pass your own extract function to receive.","title":"Custom Receiver Output"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_output/#How-it-works","page":"Custom Receiver Output","text":"receive runs the acquire → track → decode → PVT pipeline and, each processed chunk, calls extract(receiver_state) on the full ReceiverState and puts the result on the returned channel. The default is default_data_of_interest, which builds the ReceiverDataOfInterest. Because your function decides the payload, the channel's element type is inferred from what it returns (so the channel stays type-stable), and consumers like collect_data and save_data work unchanged.\n\nwarning: `extract` must be read-only and return an immutable value\nIt runs inside the tracking loop on a ReceiverState that the next chunk mutates in place (this in-place reuse is what makes the receiver allocation-free in steady state). Copy what you need into a fresh, immutable value — a named tuple, a Dict, or your own struct — and never return a reference into the state, or a later chunk will change it underneath your consumer.","title":"How it works"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_output/#What's-in-the-ReceiverState","page":"Custom Receiver Output","text":"The argument handed to extract is a ReceiverState. The fields you will usually read are:\n\nField What it holds Useful accessors\ntrack_state Tracking's per-satellite loop state, grouped by signal get_sat_states(track_state, group_key), then per sat_state: get_prn, get_carrier_doppler, get_code_phase, get_carrier_phase, estimate_cn0 (from Tracking)\nreceiver_sat_states A NamedTuple keyed by signal id (:GPSL1CA, …); each value is a Dictionaries.Dictionary{Int,ReceiverSatState} keyed by PRN GNSSReceiver.is_sat_healthy(…​.decoder), plus the decoded ephemeris in …​.decoder (from GNSSDecoder); GNSSReceiver.is_in_lock(…) and GNSSReceiver.is_ranging_ready(…) for the lock stage (see Acquisition & Tracking Parameters)\npvt Current PVT solution fields of PositionVelocityTime.PVTSolution\nruntime Elapsed signal time —\n\nThe tracking-group keys are exactly keys(receiver_sat_states) — one per constellation / signal (e.g. :GPSL1CA, :GalileoE1B). To visit every tracked satellite, iterate the group keys and, for each, the sat_states of that group:\n\nfor group_key in keys(receiver_state.receiver_sat_states)\n    for sat_state in get_sat_states(receiver_state.track_state, group_key)\n        prn = get_prn(sat_state)\n        # get_carrier_doppler(sat_state), get_code_phase(sat_state), …\n    end\nend\n\nKey any per-satellite payload by (group_key, prn) — the same key sat_data and pvt.sats use, so a satellite lines up across constellations and bands.","title":"What's in the ReceiverState"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_output/#Example","page":"Custom Receiver Output","text":"The example below emits, per satellite, the raw carrier Doppler and code phase (from the tracking loops) and the decoded health flag (from the decoder) — none of which is in the default summary. It runs on the same public recording as the Worked Example (Real Data); the download is skipped if that page already fetched it.\n\nusing Downloads, GNSSReceiver, GNSSSignals, Unitful, Tracking\n\n# Same recording as the worked example (downloaded once, then cached on disk).\nurl = \"https://sdr.ion.org/RTL_SDR/RTLSDR_Bands-L1.uint8\"\nfile = joinpath(tempdir(), \"RTLSDR_Bands-L1_prefix.uint8\")\nnbytes = 193_000_000\nif !isfile(file) || filesize(file) < nbytes\n    Downloads.download(url, file; headers = [\"Range\" => \"bytes=0-$(nbytes - 1)\"])\nend\n\nsampling_freq = 2.048e6u\"Hz\"\nsystem = GPSL1CA()\nnum_samples = Int(upreferred(sampling_freq * 4u\"ms\"))\nnothing # hide\n\nDefine an extract that copies the quantities of interest into a plain named tuple:\n\nfunction tracking_details(receiver_state)\n    track_state = receiver_state.track_state\n    receiver_sat_states = receiver_state.receiver_sat_states\n    sats = Dict(\n        (group_key, get_prn(sat_state)) => (\n            doppler = get_carrier_doppler(sat_state),\n            code_phase = get_code_phase(sat_state),\n            healthy = GNSSReceiver.is_sat_healthy(\n                receiver_sat_states[group_key][get_prn(sat_state)].decoder,\n            ),\n        )\n        for group_key in keys(receiver_sat_states)\n        for sat_state in get_sat_states(track_state, group_key)\n    )\n    return (; runtime = receiver_state.runtime, sats)\nend\n\nPass it to receive and collect the run. Note the channel's element type is now our named tuple, not a ReceiverDataOfInterest:\n\ndata_channel = receive(\n    read_uint8_iq_file(file, num_samples; center = 127.5, type = ComplexF32),\n    system,\n    sampling_freq;\n    pvt_approximate_year = 2017,\n    extract = tracking_details,\n)\n\neltype(data_channel)\n\nsnapshots = collect_data(data_channel)   # a Vector of our named tuples\nfinal = last(snapshots)\n\nfor (signal_id, prn) in sort(collect(keys(final.sats)))\n    s = final.sats[(signal_id, prn)]\n    println(\n        signal_id, \" PRN \", lpad(prn, 2),\n        \"   carrier Doppler \", round(ustrip(u\"Hz\", s.doppler); digits = 1), \" Hz\",\n        \"   code phase \", round(s.code_phase; digits = 1),\n        \"   healthy \", s.healthy,\n    )\nend","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_output/#Persisting-and-plotting","page":"Custom Receiver Output","text":"Because the channel simply carries whatever extract returns, the usual consumers work without change:\n\ncollect_data gathers the run into a Vector (as above) for analysis.\nsave_data writes it to a JLD2 file.\nget_gui_data_channel + gui expect the default ReceiverDataOfInterest, so keep the default extract when you want the live GUI.","title":"Persisting and plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"custom_output/#Defining-a-typed-payload","page":"Custom Receiver Output","text":"For a long-lived pipeline you may prefer a concrete struct over a named tuple — it documents the payload and keeps the channel element type explicit:\n\nstruct DopplerSnapshot\n    runtime::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n    dopplers::Dict{Tuple{Symbol,Int},typeof(1.0u\"Hz\")}\nend\n\nfunction doppler_only(receiver_state)\n    track_state = receiver_state.track_state\n    dopplers = Dict(\n        (group_key, get_prn(sat_state)) => get_carrier_doppler(sat_state)\n        for group_key in keys(receiver_state.receiver_sat_states)\n        for sat_state in get_sat_states(track_state, group_key)\n    )\n    return DopplerSnapshot(receiver_state.runtime, dopplers)\nend\nnothing # hide\n\nPassing extract = doppler_only then gives you a channel — and a collect_data result — of DopplerSnapshot.","title":"Defining a typed payload"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Running-the-receiver","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Running the receiver"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Signals","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Signals"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Reading-and-recording-samples","page":"API Reference","text":"The lower-level write_to_file (write raw samples straight to disk) is re-exported from SignalChannels; see that package for its documentation.","title":"Reading and recording samples"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Consuming-the-results","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Consuming the results"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Data-types","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Data types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Lock-detection","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Lock detection"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Beamforming","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Beamforming"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Internals","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Internals"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.receive","page":"API Reference","text":"receive(measurement_channel, systems, sampling_freq; num_ants = NumAnts(1), kwargs...)\nreceive(measurement_channels::Tuple, systems_per_band::Tuple, sampling_freq; kwargs...)\n\nRun the full acquire → track → decode → PVT pipeline over the samples arriving on measurement_channel and return a channel of per-chunk ReceiverDataOfInterest.\n\nsystems is a single GNSS system, a CombinedSignal pilot+data pair, or a tuple of these that share one RF band and sample stream; every constellation is acquired, tracked and decoded, and all are fused into a single multi-GNSS PVT solution. The multi-band method takes a tuple of measurement channels (one per RF band), a tuple of per-band system groups and a tuple of interm_freqs, all aligned band-by-band, and fuses every band into one solution with per-constellation clock biases and per-band inter-frequency biases. The band channels must deliver equal-length frames from one shared time base (e.g. a single capture split by front-end channel) so one frame per band stays aligned each step. The number of antenna channels in each SignalChannel must equal N in num_ants.\n\nSampled at sampling_freq, each chunk is processed by process in a spawned task; one ReceiverDataOfInterest is emitted per pvt_update_interval. Acquisition (Acquisition 2 FM-DBZP with CFAR) runs at most every acquire_every; its coherent length and Doppler resolution are derived per system from the tracking loop pull-in range, and prns restricts the search (nothing ⇒ each constellation's default range, a per-GNSS NamedTuple/Dict keyed by get_constellation_id, or a plain collection applied to every system). A satellite is declared locked once its CN0 exceeds code_lock_cn0_threshold — referred to a one-code-block record, so an N-block record locks at 10·log10(N) dB less — and contributes to the PVT solution — recomputed every pvt_update_interval — after time_in_lock_before_calculating_pvt. enable_ionospheric_correction, enable_tropospheric_correction and pvt_approximate_year (which resolves the GPS week-number rollover for old recordings) are passed through to calc_pvt.\n\nThe downconvert-and-correlator backend is auto-selected from the sample element type: Complex{Int16} inputs use Tracking's fast integer backend when max_meas (the front-end full-scale, e.g. 2^11 for a 12-bit ADC) is given, and otherwise fall back to the float CPU backend (logged once); every other element type uses the float CPU backend and ignores max_meas. Pass downconvert_and_correlator to override the choice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.receive"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.gnss_receiver_gui","page":"API Reference","text":"gnss_receiver_gui(; system = GPSL1CA(), sampling_freq = 2e6Hz, kwargs...)\n\nAcquire, track and compute a PVT solution from a live SoapySDR device and display the result in a live terminal GUI. When the stream ends the final frame stays on screen; the call blocks until you quit the dashboard with q.\n\nsystem is a single system or a tuple of systems sharing one RF band (see receive / CombinedSignal). The device selected by dev_args is configured for sampling_freq, tuned to the shared band's centre frequency and streamed for run_time — all handled by SignalChannels' stream_data, which buffers a few seconds of signal for acquisition headroom and rechunks to the chunk_time processing length. Set gain to a fixed value or leave it nothing for automatic gain control; antenna, interm_freq and num_ants override the RF front end and antenna-channel count (one identical channel config is used per antenna). Samples are streamed as stream_type (default ComplexF32); pass stream_type = Complex{Int16} to use Tracking's fast integer backend on an Int16-native device, in which case max_meas (the front-end full-scale) is required. It is ignored for float stream types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.gnss_receiver_gui"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.ReceiverState","page":"API Reference","text":"ReceiverState(T, systems; num_samples_for_acquisition, num_ants = NumAnts(1), kwargs...)\n\nBuild the initial receiver state for tracking systems, where T is the element type of the incoming signal samples (e.g. ComplexF64 or Complex{Int16}).\n\nsystems is a single GNSS system, a CombinedSignal pilot+data pair, or a tuple of these sharing one RF band; each becomes a tracking group in a single TrackState, keyed by its ranging signal's id. num_samples_for_acquisition sizes the acquisition sample buffer, and num_ants selects single- versus multi-antenna processing. doppler_estimator pins the tracking loops' estimator (it must match the one whose pull-in range sizes acquisition). One ReceiverState spans every band; pass the per-band system tuples and pre-built acquisition buffers to the primary constructor for the multi-band case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.ReceiverState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.CombinedSignal","page":"API Reference","text":"CombinedSignal(pilot, data)\n\nA pilot + data signal pair tracked jointly in one group off a shared carrier/code loop: the receiver ranges on the dataless pilot (its prompt has no navigation-bit transitions to squash the discriminator) and decodes the navigation message from the data component — e.g. CombinedSignal(GalileoE1C(), GalileoE1B()) or CombinedSignal(GPSL5Q(), GPSL5I()).\n\nPass it anywhere receive / ReceiverState take a signal system; a plain AbstractGNSSSignal (e.g. GPSL1CA()) is the data-only alternative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.CombinedSignal"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.read_files","page":"API Reference","text":"read_files(files, num_samples, end_condition = nothing; type = Complex{Int16},\n           read_measurement! = read_measurement!)\n\nReturn a SignalChannel that yields num_samples-long chunks read from files, one file per antenna channel (a single path is treated as one channel). Samples are read as type elements.\n\nThe read runs on a spawned task that stops when end_condition is reached: an Integer sample count, a notified Base.Event, or end-of-file (nothing reads until EOF). Each chunk is a freshly allocated buffer, since the lock-free channel may still hold a previously enqueued chunk.\n\nA custom read_measurement!(streams, chunk) can be supplied to decode packed or otherwise-encoded sample formats; see the note above for the contract it must honour.\n\nPass sample_rate (the recording's sampling frequency) to replay in real time — chunks are then released on a fixed schedule, one chunk's worth of signal time (num_samples / sample_rate) apart, so the replay neither runs ahead nor drifts behind the pace the signal was recorded at. Left nothing, chunks are pushed as fast as the pipeline consumes them.\n\nPacing is bounded below by sleep's ~1 ms resolution: a chunk period much shorter than that cannot be tracked, and replay runs slower than real time regardless.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.read_files"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.read_uint8_iq_file","page":"API Reference","text":"read_uint8_iq_file(file, num_samples, end_condition = nothing; center = 128,\n                   type = Complex{Int16})\n\nReturn a SignalChannel that yields num_samples-long chunks read from a single raw recording of interleaved 8-bit unsigned offset-binary in-phase/quadrature samples — the native sample format of many SDRs (e.g. RTL-SDR) and of public GNSS sample sets such as the ION SDR test data. Each unsigned byte pair (I, Q) is recentred on center (128 for standard offset binary, where 128 ≈ zero) and returned as a baseband sample of element type type.\n\nThis is the single-file, offset-binary counterpart to read_files, which reads samples that are already stored in the target element type. Because the recentred samples satisfy |real|, |imag| ≤ center, pass max_meas = center to receive when type is Complex{Int16} (the default) so it selects Tracking's fast integer downconvert-and-correlator. For a float type (e.g. ComplexF32) use center = 127.5 for exact midscale recentring and drop max_meas.\n\nend_condition stops the read exactly like read_files: an Integer sample count, a notified Base.Event, or nothing to read until end-of-file.\n\nPass sample_rate (the recording's sampling frequency) to replay in real time: chunks are then released on a fixed schedule, one chunk's worth of signal time (num_samples / sample_rate) apart, so the file plays back at the pace it was recorded — useful for watching the live gui. Left nothing, chunks are pushed as fast as the pipeline consumes them. Pacing is bounded below by sleep's ~1 ms resolution (see read_files).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.read_uint8_iq_file"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.gnss_write_to_file","page":"API Reference","text":"gnss_write_to_file(; system = GPSL1CA(), sampling_freq = 2e6Hz, run_time = 4s,\n                   dev_args = first(Devices()), output_file = \"gnss_test_data\",\n                   gain = 50dB)\n\nStream run_time of raw Complex{Int16} samples from a SoapySDR device (tuned to the shared band's centre frequency, at gain or AGC when gain === nothing) to disk, for later offline replay with read_files and receive. SignalChannels' write_to_file names each antenna channel's file \"$(output_file)$(type)$(channel).dat\" — e.g. \"gnss_test_dataComplex{Int16}1.dat\" with the defaults — so pass that full name (not output_file itself) to read_files when replaying. Blocks until the recording finishes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.gnss_write_to_file"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.get_gui_data_channel","page":"API Reference","text":"get_gui_data_channel(data_channel, push_gui_data_roughly_every = 500u\"ms\")\n\nReturn a Channel{GUIData} that downsamples data_channel for display: a spawned task consumes every ReceiverDataOfInterest but only forwards one roughly every push_gui_data_roughly_every of signal runtime (plus the very first), so the GUI is refreshed at a human rate rather than once per processed chunk.\n\nThe forwarding task also propagates a downstream close upstream: if the consumer closes the returned channel — which gui does when the user quits — it closes data_channel, ending the run instead of leaving the pipeline churning through the rest of the stream with nobody watching.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.get_gui_data_channel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.default_data_of_interest","page":"API Reference","text":"default_data_of_interest(receiver_state) -> ReceiverDataOfInterest\n\nCondense a ReceiverState into the default per-chunk payload emitted by receive: each tracked satellite's CN0, prompt correlator value and health — keyed by (signal_id, prn) — together with the current PVT solution and the runtime.\n\nThis is the default extract function of receive. Pass your own extract(receiver_state) to emit a different payload (e.g. raw carrier Doppler, code phase or decoded navigation data); the returned channel's element type is inferred from what it returns. extract runs inside the tracking loop on a ReceiverState that the next chunk mutates in place, so it must be read-only and return an immutable value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.default_data_of_interest"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.save_data","page":"API Reference","text":"save_data(data_channel; filename) -> Task\n\nConsume every element of data_channel on a spawned task and, once the channel closes, write the collected vector to filename as a JLD2 file under the key \"data_over_time\".\n\nThis is the on-disk counterpart to collect_data: like it, save_data works with the default ReceiverDataOfInterest channel from receive as well as a channel of a custom payload produced via receive's extract keyword. Returns the writer task immediately (the write happens when the producer finishes); wait on it if you need to block until the file is on disk.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.save_data"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.collect_data","page":"API Reference","text":"collect_data(data_channel) -> Vector\n\nConsume every element of data_channel, blocking until the channel closes, and return them in arrival order as a Vector of the channel's element type.\n\nThis is the in-memory counterpart to save_data: use it to gather a whole run for offline analysis or plotting (e.g. last(collect_data(data_channel)) for the final snapshot), and use save_data to persist directly to a JLD2 file instead. It works with the default ReceiverDataOfInterest channel from receive as well as a channel of a custom payload produced via receive's extract keyword.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.collect_data"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.Dashboard.gui","page":"API Reference","text":"gui(gui_data_channel; fps = 12)\n\nDisplay the receiver dashboard, consuming each GUIData from gui_data_channel. Runs a Tachikoma terminal app: a background task keeps the model fed with the latest frame while the app renders the CN0 bars, the direction-of-arrival sky plot, the Position/Velocity/Time block and an OpenStreetMap map of the fix. When the stream ends the last frame stays on screen (flagged \"stream ended\"); the app blocks until the user quits (q or Ctrl-C). Keys: d toggles the PVT diagnostics; +/- zoom and hjkl pan the map, 0 recenters it.\n\nQuitting closes gui_data_channel, which tears the pipeline down behind it (see get_gui_data_channel) — q stops the run, it does not just hide it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.Dashboard.gui"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.ReceiverDataOfInterest","page":"API Reference","text":"ReceiverDataOfInterest\n\nSnapshot of the receiver after a processed chunk: sat_data maps each tracked satellite to its SatelliteDataOfInterest, pvt is the current PVT solution and runtime is the elapsed signal time. This is the element type produced by receive.\n\nsat_data is a Dictionaries.Dictionary (GNSSReceiver.jl PR #96) keyed by (signal_id, prn) — the ranging signal's id and PRN — so the same PRN can appear on several constellations and bands without colliding, and the key matches pvt.sats (which PositionVelocityTime keys by the same ranging signal).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.ReceiverDataOfInterest"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.SatelliteDataOfInterest","page":"API Reference","text":"SatelliteDataOfInterest\n\nPer-satellite summary emitted for each processed chunk: the estimated carrier-to-noise density ratio cn0, the latest fully integrated prompt correlator value (a scalar for single-antenna, an SVector for multi-antenna), whether the satellite reports itself is_healthy, and whether its tracking loops have settled enough to range on (is_ranging_ready).\n\nis_healthy and is_ranging_ready are independent, and neither implies the other: is_healthy is what the satellite says about itself in its navigation message, while is_ranging_ready is what the receiver says about its own loops. Both must hold for the satellite to contribute to the PVT solution.\n\nEvery satellite present in a chunk's sat_data is in lock — remove_lost_satellites drops the others before the payload is built — so there is no separate is_in_lock field. What is_ranging_ready distinguishes is the stage within lock: the acquisition → tracking handover is deliberately tolerated (the point is to keep a converging satellite rather than drop and reacquire it), so a satellite can be tracked and reported here for some time before its code phase is trustworthy enough to range on — 680 code periods for a healthy signal, up to 2000 for one whose evidence never comes cleanly. Without this field such a satellite is indistinguishable from one the PVT solve is ignoring for no reason. See is_ranging_ready and LockDwell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.SatelliteDataOfInterest"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.AbstractLockDetector","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractLockDetector\n\nSupertype for the receiver's per-satellite lock detectors. A concrete detector is advanced with update and queried with is_in_lock; a satellite is treated as locked only while both its code and carrier detectors report lock.\n\nThe detectors track elapsed signal time rather than a number of update calls, so their behaviour is independent of how the incoming signal is chunked: each update is handed the signal_duration it represents and accumulates that duration into its timers. Their timings are configured in primary code periods rather than seconds — see LockDwell for why.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.AbstractLockDetector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.LockDwell","page":"API Reference","text":"LockDwell\n\nThe out-of-lock dwell shared by the receiver's lock detectors: a timer that accumulates while the detector's statistic reports \"no usable signal\" and declares lock lost once it reaches a threshold. Both CodeLockDetector and CarrierLockDetector hold one, so the handover staging below is defined in exactly one place.\n\nWhy the timings are in code periods, not seconds\n\nTracking sizes its default loop bandwidths from the primary code period T, at B_L·T ≈ 0.018: B_L = 0.018/T for the carrier loop and B_L/18 for the code loop. So every loop time constant scales with T — 1/B_L is 55.6·T for the carrier and 1000·T for the code loop:\n\nsignal T carrier B_L code B_L 1/B_L carrier 1/B_L code\nGPS L1 C/A 1 ms 18 Hz 1 Hz 56 ms 1.0 s\nGalileo E1B 4 ms 4.5 Hz 0.25 Hz 222 ms 4.0 s\nGPS L1C-D 10 ms 1.8 Hz 0.1 Hz 556 ms 10.0 s\n\nA dwell fixed in seconds therefore means something different on every signal. The 200 ms this receiver used to allow is 200 code periods on GPS L1 C/A — enough — but only 50 on Galileo E1B and 20 on GPS L1C-D, against loops that are 4× and 10× slower. That is why lock detection failed on the slower signals while GPS L1 C/A looked fine. Counted in code periods the same number means the same thing everywhere, and the GPS L1 C/A defaults are reproduced exactly (T = 1 ms, so 200 code periods is 200 ms).\n\nWhy there are two thresholds\n\nThe acquisition → tracking handover is deliberately coarse: the acquisition Doppler bin is sized to half the loop's pull-in range, and the code phase is only resolved to the sample grid. The loops need time to pull those errors in, and until they have, the prompt correlator sits off the correlation peak and carries less power than the satellite really has — so a healthy satellite reads far below its true C/N0. How far depends on the modulation: a BPSK autocorrelation falls off as 1 − |Δτ|, so a 0.25-chip error costs 2.5 dB of prompt amplitude, but BOC(1,1)'s peak is three times narrower (1 − 3|Δτ|) and the same error leaves a quarter of the amplitude — about 12 dB. BOC signals also have the slowest code loops, so they hold that deficit for longest. Measured on synthetic handovers at this receiver's own worst-case acquisition residual, a 40 dBHz GPS L1C-D satellite handed over with a 0.25-chip error reads 24-30 dBHz for its first 2.5 s.\n\nSo the dwell has two stages:\n\npull-in, from construction: tolerates pull_in_out_of_lock_time_threshold, sized to survive the whole handover transient;\nsteady state, latched permanently once the handover is over: tolerates the tighter out_of_lock_time_threshold.\n\nTwo stages rather than one long dwell, because they answer different questions. The measured worst-case pull-in demand is 174 code periods of accumulated out-of-lock time, so a single dwell would have to be ≳ 600 to be safe — and would then take three times as long to notice a satellite that genuinely disappeared. Staging buys handover tolerance without paying for it in steady-state detection latency.\n\nThe handover counts as over on either of two conditions:\n\nconfirm_time_threshold of uninterrupted favourable evidence with nothing left on the out-of-lock clock — the satellite has demonstrably converged, so there is no reason to keep extending it handover credit;\npull_in_time_threshold has elapsed and the accumulated out-of-lock time is under the steady-state threshold — the backstop for a satellite that never quite settles.\n\nBoth are needed. Without the evidence-based one, a satellite that converged in 0.1 s would still be treated as pulling in until the timer expired, and a fade during that window would take the pull-in dwell to notice — measured at 6 s on GPS L1C-D instead of 2 s. Without the timer, a satellite that never strings together a clean run never leaves the stage.\n\nRequiring the accumulated time to be under the steady-state threshold before latching matters too: otherwise a satellite sitting comfortably inside the generous pull-in threshold would be declared lost the instant the tighter one took over.\n\nWhy ranging readiness is a separate, later latch\n\npulled_in answers \"may the dwell tighten?\" and wants to fire early — as soon as the C/N0 deficit is gone, so a fade is caught on the steady-state timescale. A pseudorange consumer needs something different and slower: the code loop to have settled. Those two conflict, and measurement shows one threshold cannot serve both.\n\nC/N0 recovers well before the code phase does — on BPSK the prompt only loses 1 − |Δτ|, so a GPS L1 C/A satellite looks healthy almost immediately while its DLL (1/B_L = 1000 code periods) is still walking the handover error in. Measured at a 0.25-chip handover error, the residual code phase after 200 code periods of clean evidence is 0.09-0.19 chip — 26 to 54 m of pseudorange error — falling to 0.007-0.09 chip, 2 to 27 m, by 600. But setting the single latch to 600 tripled the time to notice a post-handover fade on GPS L1C-D, from 2 s to 6 s, because the dwell then stayed generous for far too long.\n\nSo there are two latches over the same settled_time: confirm_time_threshold (200 code periods) tightens the dwell, and ranging_confirm_time_threshold (600) admits the satellite to ranging via is_ranging_ready. Being conservative on the second is close to free: on GPS L1 C/A it lands at 680 code periods, well inside receive's own 2 s time_in_lock_before_calculating_pvt gate, and on slower signals the extra seconds are dwarfed by the tens of seconds of subframe decoding that actually set time to first fix. It is floored at confirm_code_periods, since ranging can never be ready before the handover is over.\n\nLike the pull-in stage, ranging readiness needs a timer as well as an evidence path, and for exactly the same reason: settled_time is reset by any out-of-lock verdict, so an uninterrupted run of 600 is a bar a satellite can fail to clear indefinitely. That is not hypothetical. At the default 30 dBHz threshold MomentsCN0Estimator calls a perfectly healthy record out of lock often enough that the run is rare — measured per-4 ms-chunk rates are 0.42 at 30 dBHz, 0.23 at 31, 0.095 at 32 and 0.002 at 34 — and against any disturbance recurring on a period shorter than the run (measured for one bad chunk in every 2 through 150) the evidence path never fires while the dwell sits comfortably in lock: the satellite is tracked, pulled in, and silently absent from the PVT solve forever. Since PVT needs four satellites, that trades a degraded fix for no fix. So ranging_pull_in_time_threshold (2000 code periods) backstops it, under the same guard as pulled_in's timer — the out-of-lock clock must be within what steady state tolerates, so a channel that is genuinely failing is dropped by is_in_lock rather than admitted by the timer.\n\nTwo code-loop time constants (1/B_L = 1000 code periods) rather than anything shorter, because the backstop admits a measurement it has not seen clean evidence for, and what the DLL's convergence depends on is elapsed time, not clean-run length. At 2000 code periods the loop has had nearly three times the 680 the evidence path needs, so extrapolating the residuals measured above (0.09-0.19 chip at 200 code periods, 0.007-0.09 at 600) the handover residual the backstop admits is smaller than the 2-27 m the evidence path already accepts. What it does admit is a satellite whose C/N0 is genuinely marginal, and whose code phase therefore carries more thermal jitter: for the default 1 Hz code loop and Tracking's 1-chip early-late spacing, Kaplan & Hegarty's coherent-DLL expression gives 1σ = 0.039 chip (11 m) at 30 dBHz and 0.027 chip (8 m) at 32, against 0.004 chip (1.2 m) at 45 — inside the same band. On GPS L1 C/A the timer is 2 s, which is exactly receive's default time_in_lock_before_calculating_pvt, so there it costs nothing at all.\n\nAccumulating the ranging evidence cumulatively — paying an out-of-lock chunk back rather than resetting the run — was measured as the alternative. It is faster (median 0.79 s to ranging-ready at 32 dBHz, against the timer's 1.55 s) but leaves the same hole open at a 50% duty cycle, where the accumulator has zero net drift and never reaches the bar while the dwell stays in lock. Since being conservative here is close to free, bounding the wait beats shortening it.\n\nBefore warm_up_time_threshold no evidence is accumulated at all. This covers MomentsCN0Estimator, which reports 0 dBHz with an empty prompt buffer and +Inf dBHz with exactly one prompt and stays several dB biased until ~20 prompts have accumulated, and the carrier detector's own moving averages, which start empty.\n\nresets_on_lock selects how favourable evidence is credited. false pays the accumulated time back one signal_duration at a time. true clears it outright, which is the \"optimistic\" detector of Kaplan & Hegarty §5.11.2 — it \"decides quickly and changes its mind slowly\", so declaring loss needs a consecutive run of failures. The carrier detector uses it, because a marginal satellite's phase-lock indicator dips below threshold intermittently and a paying-back accumulator would random-walk across any threshold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.LockDwell"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.CodeLockDetector","page":"API Reference","text":"CodeLockDetector <: AbstractLockDetector\n\nDeclares code lock from the estimated carrier-to-noise density ratio. After a warm-up it accumulates out-of-lock time whenever the CN0 is below cn0_threshold (and pays it back down otherwise); lock is lost once the accumulated out-of-lock time reaches the threshold its LockDwell stage allows — generous while the loops pull the acquisition handover in, tighter in steady state.\n\nThe comparison is made on the post-integration SNR, CN0 · T, rather than on CN0 alone. T is the integration time of the record the CN0 estimate came from (Tracking's get_last_fully_integrated_integration_time), and cn0_threshold is the CN0 demanded over reference_integration_time — one primary code period. A record spanning N code blocks therefore clears the detector at 10·log10(N) dB less CN0, which is what the longer coherent integration genuinely buys: detectability is set by the post-integration SNR, not by CN0 on its own.\n\nAnchoring to the primary code period rather than to the symbol period keeps cn0_threshold meaning exactly what it meant for one-block records (the default tracking cadence), and needs no data rate — so it is also defined for pilot signals, where get_data_frequency is 0 Hz.\n\ncoherence_limit caps the credited T, and defaults to Inf·s — uncapped — because Tracking already bounds a record at the coherent limit itself: post-sync it integrates clamp(preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate, 1, blocks_per_symbol) blocks, where a symbol is the data-bit period, or the secondary-code period for a pilot. That bound is generous for a long overlay (GPS L1C-P's is 1800 blocks), so set coherence_limit when crediting a full symbol is more than the loop should be trusted with.\n\nNote that Tracking's preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate defaults to 1 and this receiver never raises it, so every record is currently one code block and the credit is exactly cn0_threshold — the N > 1 behaviour above only engages once that is plumbed through.\n\nnote: The threshold cannot usefully be lowered\nSome receivers buy handover tolerance by declaring loss at a lower CN0 than they require to declare lock (PocketSDR uses 34 dBHz to lock and 25 dBHz to drop). That is not available here: MomentsCN0Estimator reports a median of ~27.6 dBHz on pure noise at 100 prompts, so the default 30 dBHz threshold is already only ~2.4 dB above the estimator's own noise floor and a lower one would never trip. The tolerance has to come from the dwell's staging instead, which is what LockDwell does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.CodeLockDetector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.CarrierLockDetector","page":"API Reference","text":"CarrierLockDetector <: AbstractLockDetector\n\nDeclares carrier lock from the prompt correlator using the Van Dierendonck narrowband-difference / narrowband-power phase-lock indicator\n\nPLI = Σ(Iₖ² − Qₖ²) / Σ(Iₖ² + Qₖ²)\n\naccumulated over every prompt the tracking loops produce. For a prompt of amplitude A in noise of total variance σ² — post-integration SNR ρ = A²/σ² = CN0·T — at carrier phase error φ,\n\nE[Iₖ² − Qₖ²] = A²·cos(2φ)      E[Iₖ² + Qₖ²] = A² + σ²\n\nso E[PLI] = cos(2φ)·ρ/(ρ+1). Lock is declared while PLI ≥ phase_lock_threshold.\n\nThis replaces a low-pass-filtered |I|-versus-|Q| amplitude comparison (lowpass(|I|)/K2 > lowpass(|Q|) with K1 = 0.0247, K2 = 1.5 — the detector of Kaplan & Hegarty §5.11.2). Three concrete reasons:\n\nIt sees every prompt. The old detector was fed one prompt per processing chunk, discarding three of every four at the receiver's 4 ms chunk over a 1 ms code period.\nIts averaging is continuous. The old filter state was reset every 80 ms block, so the value actually tested had only reached ~39% of its asymptote — K1 = 0.0247 is specified for 20 ms epochs and gives a 40-epoch time constant, which the 20-update block truncated.\nIts expectation is known in closed form, so a threshold maps to an explicit phase error at a given C/N0. The old comparison only approaches a phase test at high SNR; at low SNR it degenerates into an implicit SNR test (at perfect phase lock it flips at ρ ≈ 0.54, i.e. ~27.4 dBHz over a 1 ms record), and that crossover is a consequence of K2 rather than something you can read off it.\n\nphase_lock_threshold defaults to 0.25, matching PocketSDR's THRES_PLI. Because the indicator saturates at ρ/(ρ+1), a threshold has to be read together with the C/N0 one: at the default 30 dBHz over a 1 ms record ρ = 1, so even perfect phase lock reads only 0.5, and 0.25 demands cos(2φ) ≥ 0.5 — |φ| ≤ 30°, comparable to the 33.7° K2 = 1.5 implied. A threshold above 0.5 would be unreachable at the C/N0 the code detector still accepts. Read as a pure sensitivity floor the default is ρ ≥ 1/3, i.e. 25.2 dBHz at a 1 ms record, so — as before — the code detector's 30 dBHz stays the binding limit and the carrier detector is left to judge phase.\n\nThe two sums are tracked as exponential moving averages sharing one gain, so the indicator is continuous. smoothing_records sets the averaging window; the default of 200 keeps the noise-only indicator's median at 0.015 and its 99th percentile at 0.14 — both well clear of the 0.25 threshold — while a 36 dBHz signal reads ~0.74.\n\nThe window is counted in records, not code periods, unlike every timing in LockDwell: the gain is applied once per prompt, and a prompt is one record spanning get_last_fully_integrated_num_code_blocks code blocks. The dwell's timings stay honest in code periods because it is driven by signal_duration, which is real elapsed time; this average has no access to that. The two coincide today only because Tracking's preferred_num_code_blocks_to_integrate is 1 and this receiver never raises it (see CodeLockDetector) — once that is plumbed through, a 200-record window will be 200·N code periods long.\n\nThose noise-only figures are asymptotic, and the dwell's 80-code-period warm-up is shorter than the 200-record window, so the first judgement is made on an average that has not converged. Measured over 4000 noise realizations at the default window, the p99 settles at 0.12-0.14 but is 0.26 at 80 records (max 0.38) and 0.35 at 40 — i.e. at the moment the warm-up ends a noise-only channel is above threshold about 1% of the time. The warm-up is deliberately not lengthened to match: resets_on_lock plus the 4000-code-period dwell means declaring loss needs a consecutive run of failures, so an early excursion in either direction is absorbed, and raising the carrier warm-up to 200 would push is_ranging_ready — which ANDs both detectors — from the documented 680 code periods to 800. In the other direction the early window is already tight enough: at the code-lock threshold (ρ = 1, perfect phase lock) the indicator is below 0.25 for 0.1% of realizations at 80 records and none at 200.\n\nThe dwell clears on any favourable chunk (resets_on_lock), so declaring loss needs a consecutive run of failures. Its default steady-state threshold of 4000 code periods is 4 s on GPS L1 C/A, matching both this receiver's historical value and Ward's L0 = 240 × 20 ms = 4.8 s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.CarrierLockDetector"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.is_in_lock","page":"API Reference","text":"is_in_lock(lock_detector::AbstractLockDetector)\n\nReturn true while the detector's accumulated out-of-lock time is below the threshold its current stage allows (see LockDwell).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.is_in_lock"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.has_pulled_in","page":"API Reference","text":"has_pulled_in(lock_detector::AbstractLockDetector)\n\nWhether the detector has left its pull-in stage — the acquisition → tracking handover is over and the satellite is being held to steady-state standards (see LockDwell).\n\nThis is about the dwell, not about measurement quality: it fires as soon as the C/N0 deficit of the handover transient has cleared, which on a BPSK signal happens well before the code loop has settled. Use is_ranging_ready to decide whether to range on the satellite.\n\nThe receiver itself gates nothing on this; it is exposed for introspection and diagnostics — \"which stage is this channel in?\" — while is_ranging_ready is what actually gates ranging (see collect_pvt_sat_states!) and is_in_lock what gates tracking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.has_pulled_in"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.is_ranging_ready","page":"API Reference","text":"is_ranging_ready(lock_detector::AbstractLockDetector)\n\nWhether the tracking loops have settled enough for this satellite's measurements to be trustworthy — the question a pseudorange consumer has to ask.\n\nis_in_lock answers \"should this satellite still be tracked?\" and is deliberately tolerant through the handover, because the point is to keep a converging satellite rather than drop and reacquire it. But while the loops are still walking the coarse acquisition estimate in, the code phase — and so the pseudorange — carries a converging bias, measured at tens of metres. This latches after ranging_confirm_time_threshold of uninterrupted good evidence or, for a satellite whose evidence is never uninterrupted, once ranging_pull_in_time_threshold of signal has elapsed with the out-of-lock clock inside what steady state tolerates. It never un-latches: a satellite whose loops settled once has settled, and later disturbances are is_in_lock's business.\n\nThe timer is what keeps a marginal-but-tracked satellite from being excluded from PVT forever; see LockDwell for the measurements behind both thresholds, and for what the timer concedes in exchange (a few metres of extra code-phase jitter, not a handover bias).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.is_ranging_ready"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.phase_lock_indicator","page":"API Reference","text":"phase_lock_indicator(lock_detector::CarrierLockDetector)\n\nThe smoothed phase-lock indicator, or NaN before any prompt has been folded in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.phase_lock_indicator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.EigenBeamformer","page":"API Reference","text":"EigenBeamformer{N} <: AbstractPostCorrFilter\n\nPost-correlation filter for an N-antenna array that steers towards a satellite by eigen-beamforming. It accumulates the prompt correlator's spatial covariance and, every calc_new_every updates, recomputes the beamforming weights from the dominant eigenvector (the estimated signal subspace) before resetting the accumulator.\n\nConstruct one with EigenBeamformer(num_ants); apply it by calling the instance on a per-antenna sample vector, and evolve it with Tracking.update.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.EigenBeamformer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GNSSReceiver.process","page":"API Reference","text":"process(receiver_state, acq_plans, measurements, band_systems, sampling_freq,\n        interm_freqs = map(_ -> 0.0u\"Hz\", band_systems); kwargs...)\n\nAdvance the receiver by one measurement chunk per band and return the next ReceiverState.\n\nA single chunk runs the whole per-cycle pipeline: it (re)acquires satellites per band via acq_plans at most every acquire_every (buffering samples only when acquisition could fire), tracks every band's satellites from one TrackState, updates their lock detectors and, once enough have been locked for time_in_lock_before_calculating_pvt, recomputes the fused multi-GNSS PVT solution every pvt_update_interval. Satellites that drop out of lock are removed and reacquired with a bounded quadratic back-off. measurements, band_systems and interm_freqs are tuples aligned band-by-band (interm_freqs defaults to 0 Hz for every band); acq_plans is one NamedTuple keyed by group key across all bands. This is the function receive calls for each chunk; see it for the meaning of the remaining keyword arguments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GNSSReceiver.process"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Graphical-User-Interface","page":"Graphical User Interface","text":"GNSSReceiver.jl ships a live, interactive terminal dashboard (built on Tachikoma.jl) that updates as the receiver processes samples. It lays out four panels:\n\nCarrier-to-Noise-Density Ratio (CN0) — a bar per tracked satellite (labelled with the RINEX-style satellite id and band, e.g. G05 L1). Every satellite shown is in lock, and the colour says whether it is contributing to the fix: green when it is healthy and its tracking loops have settled enough to range on, yellow when it is healthy but still settling — normal for roughly the first second after acquisition, see the lock detection section of Acquisition & Tracking Parameters — and red when the satellite declares itself unhealthy in its navigation message.\nSatellite Direction-of-Arrival — a sky plot of the satellites in view, each drawn at its azimuth/elevation and labelled with its PRN, coloured by constellation (green GPS, blue Galileo, red GLONASS, yellow BeiDou), with a legend below. Azimuth runs clockwise from North at the top; the rings are elevation, with the zenith at the centre and the horizon on the rim, so a satellite moves outward as it sets.\nPosition Velocity Time (PVT) — the fix as time (UTC), coordinates, altitude, ground speed and heading, followed by the solution diagnostics (the full DOP breakdown — GDOP/PDOP/HDOP/VDOP/TDOP — inter-system and inter-frequency biases, and pseudorange-residual RMS). Diagnostics are shown by default; press d to hide them.\nMap — the fix on an OpenStreetMap tile (via UnicodeMaps.jl), rendered in the background. This needs network access to fetch tiles; without it (or before the first fix) the panel falls back to the coordinates and a clickable Google Maps link (an OSC 8 terminal hyperlink, so it opens on click in supporting terminals and is copy-pasteable elsewhere).\n\n(Image: The GNSSReceiver terminal GUI)","title":"Graphical User Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Keys","page":"Graphical User Interface","text":"Key Action\nq / Ctrl-C quit\nd toggle the PVT diagnostics\n+ / - zoom the map in / out\nh j k l pan the map (west / south / north / east)\n0 recenter the map on the fix and reset the zoom","title":"Keys"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Launching-the-GUI","page":"Graphical User Interface","text":"","title":"Launching the GUI"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Live-from-an-SDR","page":"Graphical User Interface","text":"The simplest path is gnss_receiver_gui, which opens the device, runs the pipeline and blocks on the GUI until the run finishes:\n\nusing SoapyRTLSDR_jll\nusing GNSSReceiver, GNSSSignals, Tracking, SoapySDR, Unitful\n\ngnss_receiver_gui(;\n    system = GPSL1CA(),\n    sampling_freq = 2e6u\"Hz\",\n    chunk_time = 4u\"ms\",\n    run_time = 40u\"s\",\n    num_ants = Tracking.NumAnts(1),\n    dev_args = first(Devices()),\n)","title":"Live from an SDR"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#From-a-data-channel","page":"Graphical User Interface","text":"If you already have a data channel from receive (replaying a file, say), wrap it with get_gui_data_channel and pass the result to gui. get_gui_data_channel down-samples the per-chunk stream to a human refresh rate:\n\nusing GNSSReceiver, GNSSSignals, Unitful\n\nmeasurement_channel = read_uint8_iq_file(\n    \"RTLSDR_Bands-L1.uint8\",\n    Int(upreferred(2.048e6u\"Hz\" * 4u\"ms\"));\n    center = 127.5,\n    type = ComplexF32,\n    sample_rate = 2.048e6u\"Hz\",    # replay in real time so the dashboard is watchable\n)\ndata_channel = receive(\n    measurement_channel, GPSL1CA(), 2.048e6u\"Hz\";\n    pvt_approximate_year = 2017,   # this recording is from 2017 (GPS week rollover)\n)\n\nGNSSReceiver.gui(get_gui_data_channel(data_channel))   # press `q` to quit\n\nQuitting with q closes the GUI channel, and that close propagates back up the pipeline: receive stops processing and the file reader stops reading. So q ends the run — it does not leave the receiver grinding through the rest of the recording in the background.\n\nFor a complete, copy-pasteable walkthrough — downloading a public recording and replaying it into the dashboard — see the Worked Example (Real Data) page.","title":"From a data channel"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Showing-the-GUI-*and*-saving-the-data","page":"Graphical User Interface","text":"The GUI is just one consumer of the data channel. To display it and persist the run, split the channel with tee and give each branch its own consumer:\n\nusing SignalChannels: tee\n\ndata_channel1, data_channel2 = tee(data_channel)\ndata_task = save_data(data_channel1; filename = \"run.jld2\")\n\ngui_channel = get_gui_data_channel(data_channel2)\nGNSSReceiver.gui(gui_channel)\n\nwait(data_task)   # `save_data` returns its writer task; wait until the file is on disk","title":"Showing the GUI and saving the data"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Refresh-rate","page":"Graphical User Interface","text":"gui accepts an fps keyword (default 12) that sets the dashboard's redraw rate; the underlying GUIData stream is already down-sampled to a human refresh rate by get_gui_data_channel. For the smoothest display, start Julia with an interactive thread (julia -t auto,1) — gui runs its render loop on the interactive threadpool when one is available, so it is never starved by the streaming/DSP work. See the docstrings for get_gui_data_channel and gui in the API Reference.","title":"Refresh rate"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"This page introduces the building blocks of GNSSReceiver.jl and shows the three ways to feed it with samples: a live SDR, a file already stored in the sample element type, and a raw 8-bit offset-binary I/Q recording.\n\nThroughout, receive's second argument is a system: a single GNSS signal such as GPSL1CA(), a CombinedSignal pilot+data pair, or a tuple of these that share one RF band (e.g. (GPSL1CA(), GalileoE1B())) — every constellation in the tuple is acquired, tracked and decoded and all are fused into a single multi-GNSS PVT solution. The examples below use a single system; see Acquisition & Tracking Parameters for the multi-constellation and multi-band forms. Note that the full-CBOC GalileoE1B() needs a sampling frequency above ~12.3 MHz — at lower rates use its BOC(1,1) approximation GalileoE1B_BOC11() (details on the parameters page).","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#The-pipeline-in-one-picture","page":"Getting Started","text":"At the centre of the package is receive. It consumes a measurement channel — a lock-free channel of sample matrices (one column per antenna) — and returns a data channel of ReceiverDataOfInterest snapshots, one per processed chunk:\n\nmeasurement channel ──▶ receive(...) ──▶ data channel ──┬─▶ get_gui_data_channel ─▶ gui\n   (raw samples)                    (PVT + per-sat CN0)  ├─▶ save_data   (to a JLD2 file)\n                                                         └─▶ collect_data (to a Vector)\n\nEverything runs on spawned tasks, so receive returns immediately and the pipeline processes samples as they arrive. A data-channel snapshot carries:\n\nsat_data — a Dictionaries.Dictionary mapping each tracked satellite, keyed by (signal_id, prn) (e.g. (:GPSL1CA, 5)), to its SatelliteDataOfInterest (carrier-to-noise ratio, prompt correlator value, health flag). Keying by (signal_id, prn) lets the same PRN appear on several constellations or bands without colliding, and matches pvt.sats;\npvt — the current PVT solution (position, velocity, time, DOP, …);\nruntime — the elapsed signal time.","title":"The pipeline in one picture"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Consuming-the-results","page":"Getting Started","text":"You typically hand the data channel to exactly one of three consumers:\n\nConsumer What it does\nget_gui_data_channel → gui Live terminal display (see Graphical User Interface).\nsave_data Consume on a background task and write a JLD2 file when the stream ends.\ncollect_data Block until the stream ends and return a Vector for offline analysis/plotting.\n\nWant more than one? Split the channel with SignalChannels.tee and attach a consumer to each branch (see Graphical User Interface for an example).","title":"Consuming the results"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#1.-Receiving-live-from-an-SDR","page":"Getting Started","text":"The one-call convenience gnss_receiver_gui opens a SoapySDR device, configures it, runs receive and shows the GUI:\n\nusing SoapyRTLSDR_jll          # or SoapyLMS7_jll, SoapyBladeRF_jll, SoapyUHD_jll, …\nusing GNSSReceiver, GNSSSignals, Tracking, SoapySDR, Unitful\n\ngnss_receiver_gui(;\n    system = GPSL1CA(),\n    sampling_freq = 2e6u\"Hz\",\n    chunk_time = 4u\"ms\",           # processing chunk length (tracking granularity)\n    run_time = 40u\"s\",\n    num_ants = Tracking.NumAnts(1),\n    dev_args = first(Devices()),   # pick the first attached device\n)\n\nsystem here can also be a tuple of systems sharing one RF band, e.g. system = (GPSL1CA(), GalileoE1B()), to receive several constellations at once.\n\nTo record raw samples now and process them later, use gnss_write_to_file.","title":"1. Receiving live from an SDR"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#2.-Replaying-a-file-stored-in-the-sample-element-type","page":"Getting Started","text":"If your recording already holds samples you can read with read_files, pass one path per antenna channel. Read them as ComplexF32 so the default CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator() handles them with no extra configuration:\n\nusing GNSSReceiver, GNSSSignals, Tracking, Unitful\nusing Unitful: Hz, ms\n\nsystem = GPSL1CA()\nsampling_freq = 5e6u\"Hz\"\nfiles = map(i -> \"antenna$i.dat\", 1:4)     # one file per antenna channel\n\n# The chunk length sets the processing/tracking granularity. A few milliseconds is a\n# sensible default.\nnum_samples = Int(upreferred(sampling_freq * 4ms))\n\nmeasurement_channel = read_files(files, num_samples; type = ComplexF32)\n\ndata_channel = receive(\n    measurement_channel,\n    system,\n    sampling_freq;\n    num_ants = NumAnts(4),\n)\n\ngui_channel = get_gui_data_channel(data_channel)\nGNSSReceiver.gui(gui_channel)      # watch the GUI update live\n\nnote: Sample element type and the correlator backend\nreceive picks its downconvert-and-correlator from the default CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator(), which works with float samples (e.g. ComplexF32). If a recording is stored in an integer type such as Complex{Int16}, read it as ComplexF32 (read_files(...; type = ComplexF32) above) — no per-front-end full-scale value is needed.","title":"2. Replaying a file stored in the sample element type"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#3.-Reading-a-raw-8-bit-offset-binary-recording","page":"Getting Started","text":"Many SDRs and public sample sets store samples as interleaved 8-bit unsigned offset-binary I/Q bytes (128 ≈ zero). read_uint8_iq_file reads that format directly, recentring each byte and emitting baseband samples — so you don't have to write the byte-unpacking loop yourself. Read as ComplexF32 (with center = 127.5 for exact midscale recentring) so the default correlator backend applies:\n\nusing GNSSReceiver, GNSSSignals, Unitful\n\nsampling_freq = 2.048e6u\"Hz\"\nnum_samples = Int(upreferred(sampling_freq * 4u\"ms\"))\n\nmeasurement_channel =\n    read_uint8_iq_file(\"RTLSDR_Bands-L1.uint8\", num_samples; center = 127.5, type = ComplexF32)\n\ndata_channel = receive(measurement_channel, GPSL1CA(), sampling_freq)\n\nresults = collect_data(data_channel)   # gather the whole run for analysis\n\nThe Worked Example (Real Data) uses exactly this path on a public recording and plots a real fix.","title":"3. Reading a raw 8-bit offset-binary recording"},{"category":"section","location":"#GNSSReceiver.jl","page":"Home","text":"A software-defined GNSS (Global Navigation Satellite System) receiver written in pure Julia. GNSSReceiver.jl takes raw radio-frequency samples — streamed live from an SoapySDR device or replayed from a file — and runs the full receiver pipeline over one or several constellations and RF bands:\n\nsamples ─▶ acquisition ─▶ tracking ─▶ navigation-bit decoding ─▶ PVT solution\n\nThe result is a Position/Velocity/Time fix that you can watch update live in a terminal GUI, persist to disk, or post-process in Julia.","title":"GNSSReceiver.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"End-to-end acquire → track → decode → PVT pipeline over a lock-free sample channel.\nMulti-constellation, multi-band: pass a tuple of systems that share one RF band to fuse them (e.g. (GPSL1CA(), GalileoE1B())), or a tuple of measurement channels and system groups to fuse several bands into one solution with per-constellation clock biases and per-band inter-frequency biases.\nPilot + data combined tracking via CombinedSignal: range on the dataless pilot, decode the navigation message from the data component (e.g. Galileo E1B/E1C, GPS L5I/L5Q).\nLive reception from any SoapySDR device (RTL-SDR, LimeSDR, BladeRF, USRP, …).\nOffline replay from files, including raw 8-bit offset-binary I/Q recordings.\nMulti-antenna processing with eigen-beamforming.\nA live, interactive terminal dashboard showing per-satellite carrier-to-noise ratios, a satellite sky plot, and the computed position on an OpenStreetMap map.\nConfigurable acquisition cadence, lock-detection and PVT parameters — see Acquisition & Tracking Parameters.","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"GNSSReceiver\")\n\nOr from the Julia REPL:\n\njulia> ]\npkg> add GNSSReceiver\n\nTo receive from a real SDR you also need a SoapySDR driver for your device, e.g. SoapyRTLSDR_jll for an RTL-SDR dongle.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"Receive live from an SDR and show the GUI (see Getting Started for the full walk-through):\n\nusing SoapyRTLSDR_jll          # your device's SoapySDR driver\nusing GNSSReceiver, GNSSSignals, Tracking, SoapySDR, Unitful\n\ngnss_receiver_gui(;\n    system = GPSL1CA(),\n    sampling_freq = 2e6u\"Hz\",\n    chunk_time = 4u\"ms\",\n    run_time = 40u\"s\",\n    num_ants = Tracking.NumAnts(1),\n    dev_args = first(Devices()),\n)\n\nDon't have an SDR handy? The Worked Example (Real Data) page downloads a public GNSS recording and computes a real position fix — and it runs automatically every time these docs are built.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"Getting Started — the building blocks (receive, get_gui_data_channel, save_data) and how to feed the receiver from a device or a file.\nAcquisition & Tracking Parameters — every knob you can turn (acquisition cadence, PRN search, lock thresholds, correlator, PVT options, …) and how to set it.\nWorked Example (Real Data) — a complete, runnable end-to-end fix on real data.\nCustom Receiver Output — emit your own per-chunk payload (raw carrier Doppler, code phase, decoded data, …) instead of the default summary.\nGraphical User Interface — the live terminal GUI.\nAPI Reference — every exported function.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Acquisition-and-Tracking-Parameters","page":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters","text":"Almost everything about how the receiver behaves is controlled through keyword arguments to receive (and, for the live path, gnss_receiver_gui). This page groups those keywords by pipeline stage and shows how to set them. The full, authoritative list lives in the receive docstring at the bottom of the page.","title":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Systems:-multi-constellation-and-multi-band","page":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters","text":"receive's second argument selects which signals to receive:\n\nA single system — one GNSS signal (GPSL1CA()), a CombinedSignal pilot+data pair, or a tuple of these — that shares one RF band:\nreceive(measurement_channel, GPSL1CA(), sampling_freq)\nA tuple of systems sharing one RF band, fused into a single multi-GNSS PVT solution. GPS L1 C/A and Galileo E1 both live on L1, so they can share one stream:\nreceive(measurement_channel, (GPSL1CA(), GalileoE1B()), sampling_freq)\n(All systems in the tuple must share one RF band; mixing bands — e.g. L1 and L5 — throws.)\nnote: Galileo E1B needs a high sampling frequency — or its BOC(1,1) approximation\nGalileoE1B() is the full CBOC(6,1,1/11) signal, whose BOC(6,1) component requires a sampling frequency above ~12.3 MHz (Nyquist for the 6.138 MHz subcarrier). At typical low SDR rates — like the 2.048 MHz used in the examples on these pages — use GalileoE1B_BOC11() instead, the BOC(1,1) approximation that ignores the BOC(6,1) component and correlates fine at low rates (at a small C/N₀ penalty).\nFor several RF bands, use the multi-band method: a tuple of measurement channels (one per band), a tuple of per-band system groups and a tuple of interm_freqs, all aligned band-by-band. Every band is fused into one solution with per-constellation clock biases and per-band inter-frequency biases:\nreceive(\n    (l1_channel, l5_channel),\n    ((GPSL1CA(),), (CombinedSignal(GPSL5Q(), GPSL5I()),)),\n    sampling_freq;\n    interm_freqs = (0.0u\"Hz\", 0.0u\"Hz\"),\n)\n\nA CombinedSignal(pilot, data) tracks the dataless pilot (which the loops range on) and the data component (whose navigation message is decoded) together in one group.","title":"Systems: multi-constellation and multi-band"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Acquisition","page":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters","text":"Acquisition is the search that finds which satellites are visible and gives a first estimate of their code phase and Doppler. It re-runs at most every acquire_every of signal time. Its coherent integration length and Doppler resolution are no longer user knobs — they are derived internally, per system, from the tracking loop's carrier-Doppler pull-in range, so that the worst-case post-acquisition residual always lands inside the loop's capture range. A detection is accepted purely on a CFAR test (constant false-alarm rate) at a fixed internal false-alarm probability — there is no acquisition CN0 threshold.\n\nKeyword Default Meaning\nacquire_every 10u\"s\" How often (in signal time) acquisition re-runs to look for new satellites.\nprns nothing Which PRNs to search for. nothing ⇒ each constellation's default range; a per-GNSS NamedTuple/Dict keyed by constellation; or a plain collection applied to every system.","title":"Acquisition"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Restricting-the-PRN-search","page":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters","text":"prns narrows (or widens) the satellites acquisition searches for. For a multi-GNSS run you can give a per-constellation list keyed by constellation name:\n\ndata_channel = receive(\n    measurement_channel, (GPSL1CA(), GalileoE1B()), sampling_freq;\n    prns = (GPS = [1, 8, 30], Galileo = [3, 9, 24]),\n)\n\nA plain collection is applied to every system, and nothing uses each constellation's default range. Each system's search is further restricted to the PRNs that actually broadcast its signal.","title":"Restricting the PRN search"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Front-end-and-correlator","page":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters","text":"Keyword Default Meaning\nnum_ants NumAnts(1) Number of antenna channels. Must match the columns of the measurement channel(s).\ninterm_freq / interm_freqs 0.0u\"Hz\" Intermediate frequency of the incoming samples (single-band interm_freq; a tuple interm_freqs for the multi-band method).\ndownconvert_and_correlator auto (by element type) The correlator backend. nothing auto-selects: Tracking's fast integer backend for Complex{Int16} samples, the float CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator() otherwise. Pass one explicitly to override.\nmax_meas nothing Front-end full-scale (largest |real|/|imag| of any sample, e.g. 2^11 for a 12-bit ADC). Required for Complex{Int16} samples (the integer backend); ignored for float samples or when downconvert_and_correlator is given.\n\nThe correlator backend is auto-selected from the sample element type: Complex{Int16} recordings use Tracking's fast integer downconvert-and-correlator (which needs max_meas), and every other element type uses the float CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator(). You can therefore either pass Complex{Int16} samples with max_meas, or read integer recordings as ComplexF32 (read_uint8_iq_file(...; center = 127.5, type = ComplexF32)) to use the float backend with no full-scale value. See Getting Started.","title":"Front end & correlator"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Lock-detection","page":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters","text":"A satellite is tracked only while it is in lock. Lock is declared per satellite by a CodeLockDetector — a threshold on the post-integration SNR — and a CarrierLockDetector — the narrowband difference/power phase-lock indicator Σ(I²−Q²)/Σ(I²+Q²). Both track elapsed signal time, so their behaviour is independent of how the signal is chunked.\n\nThe lock knob surfaced directly on receive is the code-lock CN0 threshold:\n\nKeyword Default Meaning\ncode_lock_cn0_threshold nothing (⇒ 30u\"dBHz\" per signal) A satellite is declared code-locked while its estimated CN0 stays above this, referred to a one-code-block record.\n\ndata_channel = receive(\n    measurement_channel, GPSL1CA(), 2.048e6u\"Hz\";\n    code_lock_cn0_threshold = 32u\"dBHz\",   # stricter lock\n)\n\nThe threshold is referred to a record spanning one primary code period, because what decides detectability is the post-integration SNR CN0 · T rather than CN0 on its own. When tracking integrates over N code blocks per record, a satellite therefore holds lock at 10·log10(N) dB less CN0 — 3 dB at two blocks — since the longer coherent integration recovers exactly that much. Anchoring to the code period rather than to the symbol period keeps the number meaning the same thing for the default one-block cadence and stays defined for pilot signals, which have no data rate.","title":"Lock detection"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Two-stages:-handover,-then-steady-state","page":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters","text":"The acquisition → tracking handover is deliberately coarse — the acquisition Doppler bin is half the tracking loop's pull-in range, and the code phase is only resolved to the sample grid. Until the loops have pulled those errors in, the prompt correlator sits off the correlation peak and reports less power than the satellite really has, so a perfectly healthy satellite looks weak. A 0.25-chip code-phase error costs 2.5 dB of prompt amplitude on BPSK (GPS L1 C/A, autocorrelation 1 − |Δτ|) but about 12 dB on BOC(1,1) (GPS L1C, 1 − 3|Δτ|), whose correlation peak is three times narrower — and BOC signals also have the slowest code loops, so they hold that deficit longest.\n\nBoth detectors therefore run a pull-in stage with a generous out-of-lock allowance, latching into a tighter steady state once the satellite has either strung together enough good evidence to be considered converged or exhausted its pull-in window. This buys handover tolerance without making a genuine signal loss slower to notice.\n\nRanging is gated separately from tracking, and on a later criterion. A satellite in its pull-in stage is still kept — that is the point — but it does not contribute to the PVT solution until its loops have settled, because a code phase that is still converging produces a pseudorange biased by metres to tens of metres. time_in_lock_before_calculating_pvt alone would not achieve this: it counts from the handover, so it can elapse while the loops are still settling.\n\nThat stage is observable rather than hidden: each satellite's SatelliteDataOfInterest carries is_ranging_ready, and the dashboard's CN0 panel draws a still-settling satellite yellow rather than green (see Graphical User Interface). Without that, a satellite held through the handover is indistinguishable on screen from one the PVT solve is ignoring for no reason.\n\nThose two are deliberately separate thresholds rather than one, because they pull in opposite directions. Tightening the dwell wants to happen early, so that a fade after a successful handover is noticed on the steady-state timescale. Admitting a satellite to ranging wants to happen late, because C/N0 recovers long before the code loop does — on BPSK the prompt only loses 1 − |Δτ|, so a GPS L1 C/A satellite looks healthy almost immediately while its DLL is still tens of metres out. Measured: at 200 code periods of clean evidence the residual code phase is 0.09-0.19 chip (26-54 m); at 600 it is 0.007-0.09 chip (2-27 m). Using one threshold for both meant either admitting the 54 m bias or tripling the fade-detection delay.\n\nRanging readiness needs a timer as well as an evidence path, for the same reason the pull-in stage does: the evidence clock is reset by any out-of-lock verdict, so an uninterrupted run is a bar a satellite can fail to clear indefinitely. That is not hypothetical — at the default 30 dBHz threshold MomentsCN0Estimator calls a healthy record out of lock often enough that the run is rare (measured per-4 ms-chunk rates of 0.42 at 30 dBHz, 0.23 at 31, 0.095 at 32), and against any disturbance recurring on a period shorter than the run the evidence path never fires at all while the dwell sits comfortably in lock. The satellite would then be tracked, converged, and silently absent from the PVT solve forever; since PVT needs four satellites, that trades a degraded fix for no fix. The backstop admits such a satellite once enough signal has elapsed — two code-loop time constants — with its out-of-lock clock still inside what steady state tolerates. What it concedes is a few metres of extra code-phase thermal jitter (1σ of 0.039 chip, 11 m, at 30 dBHz against 0.004 chip, 1.2 m, at 45), not a converging handover bias, which is inside the band the evidence path already accepts.\n\nOne residual case is left open deliberately: a satellite whose accumulated out-of-lock time parks between the steady-state and pull-in allowances — 200 to 600 code periods — never leaves the pull-in stage, and so never becomes ranging-ready either. Reaching it takes a burst of failures followed by a sustained near-50% duty cycle; under a stochastic signal the clock random-walks out of that band, either down to zero (whereupon it latches) or up past the pull-in allowance (whereupon lock is lost and the satellite is reacquired). Closing it would mean letting the pull-in stage latch while its clock is still above the steady-state threshold, which is exactly the discontinuity the latch guard exists to prevent.","title":"Two stages: handover, then steady state"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Timings-scale-with-the-code-period","page":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters","text":"Every detector timing is configured as a multiple of the ranging signal's primary code period rather than in seconds, because that is what the tracking loops' time constants scale with: Tracking sizes its default bandwidths at B_L·T ≈ 0.018, so a 1 ms code gets an 18 Hz carrier / 1 Hz code loop while a 10 ms code gets 1.8 Hz / 0.1 Hz.\n\nSignal Code period Warm-up Steady code dwell Pull-in allowance Ranging ready Ranging backstop\nGPS L1 C/A 1 ms 80 ms 200 ms 600 ms 680 ms 2 s\nGalileo E1B 4 ms 320 ms 800 ms 2.4 s 2.7 s 8 s\nGPS L1C-D 10 ms 800 ms 2 s 6 s 6.8 s 20 s\n\n\"Ranging ready\" is the evidence path — what a healthy satellite achieves; \"ranging backstop\" is the timer that bounds the wait for one whose evidence never comes cleanly.\n\nA dwell fixed in seconds means something different on every signal: the 200 ms this receiver used to allow is 200 code periods on GPS L1 C/A but only 20 on GPS L1C-D, against loops that are ten times slower — which is why lock detection failed on the slower signals while GPS L1 C/A looked fine. The GPS L1 C/A numbers are unchanged.\n\nOne consequence worth stating outright: on codes longer than 1 ms these timings, not time_in_lock_before_calculating_pvt, are what actually decide when a satellite joins the solve. A healthy GPS L1 C/A satellite is ranging-ready at 680 ms, well inside the 2 s default, so that knob still binds there. On Galileo E1B (2.7 s) and GPS L1C-D (6.8 s) it no longer does, and raising it below those figures has no effect at all. The extra seconds are in practice dwarfed by the tens of seconds of subframe decoding that set time to first fix.\n\nThe remaining detector settings — the stage timings in code periods, the carrier phase-lock threshold and its smoothing window — are set at detector construction; see the LockDwell, CodeLockDetector and CarrierLockDetector docstrings in the API Reference for the defaults and the measurements behind them.","title":"Timings scale with the code period"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#PVT","page":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters","text":"Keyword Default Meaning\ntime_in_lock_before_calculating_pvt 2u\"s\" A satellite must be locked this long before it is used for PVT.\npvt_update_interval 100u\"ms\" How often the PVT solution is recomputed (also the rate at which the data channel emits).\nenable_ionospheric_correction true Apply the broadcast ionospheric correction.\nenable_tropospheric_correction true Apply the tropospheric correction.\npvt_approximate_year current UTC year Resolves the GPS week-number rollover for old recordings.\n\npvt_approximate_year matters for archived data: an old recording processed with the wrong year lands ~19.6 years off. The Worked Example (Real Data) sets pvt_approximate_year = 2017 for its 2017 recording.\n\ndata_channel = receive(\n    measurement_channel, GPSL1CA(), 2.048e6u\"Hz\";\n    pvt_update_interval = 200u\"ms\",\n    enable_tropospheric_correction = false,\n    pvt_approximate_year = 2017,\n)","title":"PVT"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Custom-per-chunk-output","page":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters","text":"extract replaces the per-chunk payload builder; the default default_data_of_interest emits a ReceiverDataOfInterest. Pass your own extract(receiver_state) to emit anything else — see Custom Receiver Output.","title":"Custom per-chunk output"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Multi-antenna-processing","page":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters","text":"For multiple antennas (NumAnts(N) with N > 1) the post-correlation filter is an EigenBeamformer. The number of antenna channels in each measurement channel must equal N in num_ants.","title":"Multi-antenna processing"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Full-reference","page":"Acquisition & Tracking Parameters","text":"The complete, authoritative list of keyword arguments — with their exact defaults — is in the docstrings of receive and ReceiverState in the API Reference.","title":"Full reference"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Worked-Example-(Real-Data)","page":"Worked Example (Real Data)","text":"This page runs the complete receiver pipeline on a real GNSS recording and computes an actual position fix. Every code block below is executed when the documentation is built, so the numbers and plots you see were produced by GNSSReceiver.jl on live data — not hand-written.\n\nThe recording is the public ION SDR sample data: 8-bit unsigned offset-binary I/Q at 2.048 MS/s, GPS L1, captured on 2017-09-10 in Oegstgeest, NL. It is the same signal used by the package's integration test.","title":"Worked Example (Real Data)"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Download-the-recording","page":"Worked Example (Real Data)","text":"We only need the first ~47 seconds to acquire the satellites and decode enough of the navigation message for a fix, so we fetch a prefix of the file with an HTTP range request (≈ 193 MB) rather than the full 60-second, ~246 MB recording.\n\nusing Downloads\n\nurl = \"https://sdr.ion.org/RTL_SDR/RTLSDR_Bands-L1.uint8\"\nfile = joinpath(tempdir(), \"RTLSDR_Bands-L1_prefix.uint8\")\nnbytes = 193_000_000\n\nif !isfile(file) || filesize(file) < nbytes\n    Downloads.download(url, file; headers = [\"Range\" => \"bytes=0-$(nbytes - 1)\"])\nend\nfilesize(file)","title":"Download the recording"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Run-the-receiver","page":"Worked Example (Real Data)","text":"Reading the raw offset-binary file, running the pipeline and collecting the results is three calls — read_uint8_iq_file, receive and collect_data:\n\nusing GNSSReceiver, GNSSSignals, Unitful\n\nsampling_freq = 2.048e6u\"Hz\"\nsystem = GPSL1CA()\nnum_samples = Int(upreferred(sampling_freq * 4u\"ms\"))   # 4 ms chunks\n\nmeasurement_channel =\n    read_uint8_iq_file(file, num_samples; center = 127.5, type = ComplexF32)\n\ndata_channel = receive(\n    measurement_channel,\n    system,\n    sampling_freq;\n    pvt_approximate_year = 2017,   # resolves the GPS week-number rollover for old data\n)\n\nresults = collect_data(data_channel)\nlength(results)   # one snapshot per `pvt_update_interval`, plus the final state\n\nnote: Reading the raw offset-binary file\nThe raw file stores each I and Q value as an 8-bit unsigned byte (0…255) in offset-binary form: the zero-signal level sits at the middle of the range, 128, rather than at 0. So a byte of 128 means ≈ zero amplitude, 255 means maximum positive and 0 means maximum negative.read_uint8_iq_file turns that into normal signed baseband samples by recentring — subtracting center from every byte. Reading as ComplexF32 with center = 127.5 recentres exactly on midscale and produces float baseband samples, so receive's default CPUThreadedDownconvertAndCorrelator() processes them with no extra configuration.\n\nresults is a Vector of ReceiverDataOfInterest snapshots. The last one is the final state of the receiver:\n\nfinal = last(results)\nfinal.runtime","title":"Run the receiver"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Carrier-to-noise-ratio-per-satellite","page":"Worked Example (Real Data)","text":"The estimated CN0 of every tracked satellite — this is the same information the live GUI shows as a bar chart:\n\nsat_data is a Dictionaries.Dictionary keyed by (signal_id, prn), so iterate it with pairs:\n\nusing UnicodePlots\n\nlabels = String[]\ncn0s = Float64[]\nfor ((signal_id, prn), sd) in pairs(final.sat_data)\n    push!(labels, string(signal_id, \" \", prn))\n    push!(cn0s, ustrip(sd.cn0))\nend\n\nbarplot(\n    labels,\n    round.(cn0s; digits = 1);\n    xlabel = \"CN0 [dBHz]\",\n    ylabel = \"Satellite\",\n    title = \"Carrier-to-Noise-Density Ratio\",\n)","title":"Carrier-to-noise ratio per satellite"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#The-position-fix","page":"Worked Example (Real Data)","text":"Ephemeris decoding takes about half a minute, so the first PVT solution appears roughly 35 seconds into the recording. Here is the final fix:\n\nusing PositionVelocityTime: get_LLA\n\nlla = get_LLA(final.pvt)\n\nprintln(\"Time (TAI):        \", final.pvt.time)\nprintln(\"Latitude:          \", lla.lat)\nprintln(\"Longitude:         \", lla.lon)\nprintln(\"Altitude:          \", round(lla.alt; digits = 1), \" m\")\nprintln(\"Satellites in fix: \", length(final.pvt.sats))\nprintln(\"Position DOP:      \", round(final.pvt.dop.PDOP; digits = 2))\n\nThe recording was made at roughly 52.177° N, 4.490° E in Oegstgeest, Netherlands — which is exactly what the receiver reports. You can drop those coordinates straight into a map:\n\nprintln(\"https://www.google.com/maps/search/$(ustrip(lla.lat)),$(ustrip(lla.lon))\")","title":"The position fix"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#There's-more-in-each-snapshot","page":"Worked Example (Real Data)","text":"The summary is compact, but the PVT solution it carries is the full PositionVelocityTime.PVTSolution, so a lot more is available from the same snapshot without any extra work:\n\npvt = final.pvt\nprintln(\"Velocity (ECEF):     \", round.(pvt.velocity; digits = 2), \" m/s\")\nprintln(\"Clock drift:         \", pvt.relative_clock_drift)\nprintln(\"GDOP / HDOP / VDOP:  \", round(pvt.dop.GDOP; digits = 2), \" / \",\n        round(pvt.dop.HDOP; digits = 2), \" / \", round(pvt.dop.VDOP; digits = 2))\n# `sat_data` is keyed by (signal_id, prn):\nsome_key = first(keys(final.sat_data))\nprintln(\"Prompt ($some_key):  \", final.sat_data[some_key].prompt)\n\nIf you need a quantity that the summary does not carry at all — the raw carrier Doppler, code phase or carrier phase of the tracking loops, or the decoded navigation data — you can have receive emit a payload of your own. See Custom Receiver Output.","title":"There's more in each snapshot"},{"category":"section","location":"example/#Watch-it-live-in-the-terminal","page":"Worked Example (Real Data)","text":"To watch this unfold live instead of post-processing it, hand the data channel to the live Graphical User Interface instead of collect_data. Using the same file and settings as above:\n\nusing GNSSReceiver, GNSSSignals, Unitful\n\nsampling_freq = 2.048e6u\"Hz\"\nnum_samples = Int(upreferred(sampling_freq * 4u\"ms\"))\n\n# `sample_rate` paces the file replay to real time, so the dashboard updates at the speed\n# the signal was recorded instead of flashing past and closing immediately.\nmeasurement_channel = read_uint8_iq_file(\n    file, num_samples; center = 127.5, type = ComplexF32, sample_rate = sampling_freq)\ndata_channel = receive(\n    measurement_channel, GPSL1CA(), sampling_freq; pvt_approximate_year = 2017)\n\n# Opens the interactive dashboard (CN0 bars, sky plot, PVT, map). Press `q` to quit.\nGNSSReceiver.gui(get_gui_data_channel(data_channel))\n\nThis block is not run when the docs are built because the dashboard takes over the terminal. Run it in a real terminal — ideally with an interactive thread (julia -t auto,1) so the render loop is never starved. With sample_rate set the replay runs at real time, so the fix appears after the receiver has decoded enough of the navigation message (~35 s in), and the final frame stays on screen until you press q.","title":"Watch it live in the terminal"}]
}
